● Injected adaptive memory context into system prompt

● <adaptive_rules>
  - At game start KIs are evenly distributed in memory: KI i's first command is placed at floor(i *
  Anzahl Speicherzellen / Anzahl KIs).
  - KI execution always begins at the KI's first KI command that is not STOP.
  - end-game must output 'Running AIs: [names]' and 'Stopped AIs: [names]' but only include lists
  that have at least one AI; lists must be ordered ascending by initial memory position of the AIs;
  after end-game the system returns to the initialization phase.
  - Execution order is fixed by initial memory positions as KI 0, KI 1, KI 2, ... and KIs are
  executed sequentially (no parallel execution).
  - Each KI must include at least one KI command that is not STOP.
  - If set-init-mode is not used, the init mode must be INIT_MODE_STOP.
  - set-init-mode must only print "Changed init mode from [Old Mode] to [New Mode]" when the mode or
   its parameters actually changed; otherwise produce no output.
  - INIT_MODE_RANDOM must instantiate the RNG using the constructor Random(long seed).
  - INIT_MODE_RANDOM seed must be an integer between -1337 and 1337.
  - All other executed AI commands during next produce no output.
  - If an AI stops during next execution, output '[AIName] executed [Counter] steps until
  stopping.'; the STOP command itself does not count toward the printed counter.
  - next may be executed even when all AIs are stopped; such executions perform no AI commands.
  - next takes an optional argument specifying the number of AI commands to execute and defaults to
  1 if omitted.
  - For each memory cell (starting at index 0) memory initialization must use Random::nextInt(int
  bound) to pick the KI command where bound = number of KI commands.
  - show-ai output first line must be '[AIName] [RUNNING|STOPPED]@[AI counter]'; second line must be
   'Next Command: [next AI-command] @[memory cell]' and is only shown for running AIs; the AI
  counter counts how many AI commands the AI has executed since game start (possibly including
  STOP).
  - All commands must be executed against the current program state.
  - AI-bomb is an AI command that is either (a) a STOP command, (b) a JMP with entry A = 0, or (c) a
   JMZ with entry A = 0 and entry B = 0.
  - A program must contain at least one KI command.
  - After printing an error message the program must continue normally and wait for the next input
  (not crash the interactive loop).
  - After processing an input the program must wait for further inputs until terminated by `quit`.
  - Program is started with multiple arguments; if argument count or an argument is incorrect the
  program must print an error message and terminate at startup.
  - The program must terminate at any time when it receives the command 'quit' and must not rely on
  banned exit functions to implement this.
  - Use System.in for input and System.out for output unless the task states otherwise.
  - KI commands belonging to different KIs must not overlap in memory.
  - Program source code must be written in English.
  - For each memory cell memory initialization must use Random::nextInt() to generate entry A and
  set the cell's entry A to that value.
  - Maximum number of competing AIs is determined by the number of program-start arguments.
  - start-game allows the same AI to be selected multiple times and assigns duplicate-instance names
   as '[AIName]#[number]' where numbering starts at 0.
  - start-game may only start when at least two AIs have been chosen.
  - Successful start-game execution must output exactly 'Game started.'
  - The Javadoc @author tag must contain only the student u-Kürzel.
  - The first argument specifies the memory size and must be in the range [7, 1337].
  - Memory cell arguments must always be integers in the inclusive range [INT_MIN, INT_MAX].
  - Exactly 4 AI-independent symbols are defined first, then 2 symbols per AI; the number of AIs is
  determined by the number of arguments and at least 2 AIs must be defined.
  - Help output must be in English, a single line, commands listed lexicographically, format '[Name
  of command]: [Description]'.
  - Subsequent arguments define symbols for memory representation; symbols may consist of multiple
  characters but must not contain spaces or occur multiple times.
  - Avoid using Java Streams for this assignment.
  - Choose appropriate visibilities for classes, methods, and attributes.
  - Do not include personal data other than your u-Kürzel in submissions.
  - Do not place classes in the default package.
  - Do not reassign the standard input or output streams (System.in/System.out).
  - Do not use System.exit(), Runtime.exit(), or similar functions.
  - Javadoc documentation rules must be followed.
  - Rules for variable, method, and package naming must be followed.
  - Submissions must not use Java library elements except the allowed ones (java.util.Scanner and
  all elements from java.lang, java.io, java.util, java.util.regex).
  - Submissions must obey a maximum line width of 140 characters.
  - Submissions must use Java SE 17.
  - Submit only your .java files in the required folder structure and only as individual work.
  - The submitted program must compile without errors.
  - Whitespace rules specified by the guidelines and Checkstyle must be followed.
  </adaptive_rules>

  <memories>
