● <adaptive_rules>
  - After end-game execution the system must return to the initialization phase.
  - end-game must print "Running AIs: [names]" and/or "Stopped AIs: [names]" only for lists that
  contain at least one AI.
  - The Running AIs and Stopped AIs lists in end-game must be sorted ascending by the initial memory
   positions of the AIs.
  - show-ai must print first line "[AI Name] [RUNNING|STOPPED]@[AI counter]" and, if AI is running,
  a second line "Next Command: [next AI-command] @[memory cell]"; stopped AIs must not include the
  second line.
  - start-game may choose the same AI multiple times; duplicated instances are allowed.
  - start-game may only start when at least two AIs have been chosen.
  - Successful start-game must print exactly "Game started.".
  - The maximum number of AIs that can compete equals the number of command-line arguments at
  program start.
  - When an AI is instantiated multiple times its instance name must be formed as "[AI
  Name]#[instance number starting at 0]".
  - show-memory must choose symbols with this priority: (1) next command of the next AI, (2) next
  command of other AIs, (3) AI-bomb, (4) AI-command of an AI, (5) untouched since start.
  - An AI counter reported by show-ai must count how many AI-commands the AI executed since game
  start (including STOP if executed).
  - If an AI stops during execution next must print "[AI name] executed [counter] steps until
  stopping."; the STOP command does not count towards that counter; all other executed AI-commands
  produce no output.
  - next accepts an optional argument for the number of AI-commands to execute; default is 1.
  - next may be executed when all AIs are stopped; it will not execute any AI-commands in that case
  but is allowed to run.
  - show-memory membership of a command is originally the AI-commands that AI loaded at
  initialization and later changes to the AI that last processed the command in any way; AI-bomb
  classification is updated accordingly; processing does not require modifying the entry, only
  attempting to change/write the cell.
  - AI-bomb is defined as an ordinary AI-command that satisfies one of: (a) STOP command; (b) JMP
  with entry A = 0; (c) JMZ with entry A = 0 and entry B = 0.
  - show-memory must display 10 memory cells per block starting at the given cell, each line
  formatted as "[Symbol] [Index]: [AI-Command] | [Entry A] | [Entry B]" with all columns
  right-aligned and special characters (including spaces) respected.
  - Subsequent arguments define symbols for memory representation; each symbol may contain multiple
  characters but must not contain spaces.
  - The first command-line argument specifies the memory size and must be in the inclusive range [7,
   1337].
  - show-memory region boundary symbol must be inserted between two command symbols and increases
  the memory display length by twice the boundary-symbol length.
  - help output command descriptions must not contain line breaks.
  - help output descriptions must be written in English.
  - help output must list commands in ascending lexicographic order by command name.
