  <memories>
    - Code Fight simulates execution of multiple KIs in a shared memory.
    - Game start: KIs are loaded into memory, then a sequential, round-based execution runs where
  one KI command executes at a time.
    - The Code Fight assignment is inspired by the classic game Core War.
    - ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 simulates multiple KI programs running in a shared memory using a
  sequential, round-based execution model.
    - ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 defines KIs as programs: a sequence of at least one KI command;
  execution proceeds to the next memory cell by default unless a KI command changes the next
  executed cell.
    - The project ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 includes the document task.md.
    - The document task.md describes the assignment 'Abschlussaufgabe 1 — Code Fight'.
    - The document task.md contains the Code Fight assignment and its metadata (course, semester,
  instructor, group, points, version).
    - ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 USES a defined KI command set of nine commands (STOP, MOV_R, MOV_I,
  ADD, ADD_R, JMP, JMZ, CMP, SWAP) with the documented semantics for copying, arithmetic, jumps,
  comparisons, and swapping.
    - The current workspace for ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 does not contain the file examples.md
    - ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 includes the feature 'show-memory' which selects symbols by priority
  (1: next command next KI → 5: untouched) and treats KI-bombs as STOP, JMP with A=0, and JMZ with
  A=B=0.
    - ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 defines the game memory as cyclic and memory cells numbered starting
  at 0.
    - ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 USES a cyclic game memory (Speicher) composed of numbered cells with
  wrap-around starting at 0.
    - ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 includes the example 'Sleepy' program ('ADD 10 -1; MOV_I 2 -1; JMP -2
   0; STOP 13 37') which uses an ADD-based counter, MOV_I to copy a 'STOP 13 37' source into target
  cells, and JMP to loop, thereby periodically writing STOP commands to attempt to stop other KIs.
    - ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 uses a cyclic memory of numbered cells starting at 0; each cell
  stores a KI command and two entries (A and B).
    - ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 includes a help feature that prints line-by-line short descriptions
  of commands available in the current game phase.
    - ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 includes the feature 'show-memory [Index]' which displays memory
  ranges with boundary symbols and a 10-cell detailed view formatted right-aligned.
    - ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 includes the feature 'show-ai [Name]' which prints a status line plus
   the Next Command, but only for running KIs.
    - ProgrammingWT2324-Final1 USES the convention "%> " to denote a command-line program call.
  </memories>
